## 1. INTRINSIC VALUE — THE BIRDS IN THE BUSH

### 1.1 The Core Framework

**Intrinsic value =** The present value of all the cash a business will distribute between now and judgment day.

**Aesop's fable applied:** "A bird in the hand is worth two in the bush." Investing is laying out cash (a bird in the hand) today to get more cash later.

**Every investment decision answers three questions:**
1. **How many birds are in the bush?** (magnitude of future cash flows)
2. **How certain are you that they are there?** (predictability / moat durability)
3. **When will they emerge?** (time horizon)

```mermaid
graph TD
    INVEST[Lay out cash today = Bird in Hand] --> Q1{Question 1}
    Q1[How many birds in the bush?] --> MAGNITUDE[Magnitude of Future Cash Flows]
    INVEST --> Q2{Question 2}
    Q2[How certain are they there?] --> CERTAINTY[Predictability & Moat Durability]
    INVEST --> Q3{Question 3}
    Q3[When will they emerge?] --> TIMING[Time Horizon]
    MAGNITUDE --> VALUE[Intrinsic Value = PV of All Future Distributions]
    CERTAINTY --> VALUE
    TIMING --> VALUE
    VALUE --> DECIDE{Buy if price < Intrinsic Value with Margin of Safety?}
    DECIDE -->|Yes| BUY[Buy at Significant Discount]
    DECIDE -->|No| PASS[Pass - Too Hard Pile]
```

### 1.2 Discount Rate

| Common Practice | Buffett-Munger Practice |
|----------------|------------------------|
| Complex risk-adjusted WACC models | Long-term U.S. government bond rate |
| Beta-weighted discount rates | Not used — "false precision" |
| Multiple scenario DCFs | Simple conservative estimate only |

**Key principle:** If a decision requires complex math or projections to multiple decimal places, they won't make it. **A good investment should "shout at you"** without higher mathematics.

### 1.3 Margin of Safety

They demand a **significant discount** to their calculated intrinsic value. This protects against:
- Errors in estimation
- Bad luck
- Unknown unknowns

> *"When you find a wonderful business at a sensible price — buy a lot of it. Don't nibble."*

