## 7. SELL DISCIPLINE & PERMANENT PORTFOLIO

### 7.1 Three Reasons to Sell

| Reason | Example |
|--------|---------|
| **Thesis invalidation** — initial analysis was wrong | IBM — "performance over 6 years didn't match original expectations" |
| **Moat permanently eroded** — competitive advantage destroyed | Airlines (2020) — pandemic fundamentally changed industry economics |
| **Better opportunity found** — only in capital-constrained environments | Buffett in early days — "reluctantly sell something terribly cheap to buy something even cheaper" |

### 7.2 The Permanent Holdings

Buffett explicitly named these as **permanent holdings** (vowed never to sell unless forced by extreme deterioration):

1. **The Washington Post Company**
2. **Capital Cities / ABC (now part of Disney)**
3. **GEICO**
4. **Coca-Cola**

### 7.3 What Makes a Holding Permanent

- Complete confidence in management's integrity and ability
- Durable competitive advantage that's unlikely to erode
- Businesses they understand perfectly
- No pressure to sell for opportunity cost (Berkshire's scale eliminates this)

### 7.4 The Tax Factor

**Taxes do NOT drive their behavior.** Buffett: "If we were running Berkshire absent a capital gains tax, I don't think it would make much difference in what we do... we don't own them because taxes have restrained us from selling them."

Deferred taxes from unrealized gains are viewed merely as "an absence of cash that's going to be paid out until we sell those securities."

