## 8. CAPITAL ALLOCATION & BUYBACKS

### 8.1 The Golden Rule

Retain earnings ONLY if $1 retained creates >$1 of market value. If they fail this test over time, capital should be returned to shareholders.

### 8.2 Berkshire's Buyback Policy — Evolution

| Era | Trigger | Rationale |
|-----|---------|-----------|
| **Early** | 110% of book value | Hard benchmark for when stock was clearly cheap |
| **Mid** | 120% of book value | Widening gap between book and intrinsic value |
| **Current** | Both Buffett AND Munger agree stock is below intrinsic value | Requires at least $20B in cash reserves maintained |

### 8.3 When Should a Company Buy Back Stock?

Only when shares are selling at a **demonstrable discount to a conservatively calculated intrinsic value.**

If a company buys back overvalued stock → destroys shareholder value.

