## 11. BIGGEST MISTAKES

### 11.1 Berkshire Hathaway Textile Mill

| Factor | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **What** | Original textile mill acquisition |
| **Why it failed** | New England textiles = "congealed electricity" — utility rates 60% higher than competitors |
| **Outcome** | Guaranteed eventual death — no amount of hard work could save it |
| **Lesson** | A terrible business cannot be saved by cheap purchase prices or hard work |

### 11.2 Dexter Shoe

| Factor | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **What** | Paid $400M+ for a shoe company |
| **Why it failed** | Competitive advantage evaporated against foreign competition |
| **The killer** | Buffett paid for it with **Berkshire stock** |
| **Outcome** | That stock compounded to be worth billions — colossally expensive error |
| **Lesson** | **Never issue Berkshire stock for an acquisition** |

### 11.3 US Air

| Factor | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **What** | Preferred stock in an airline |
| **Why it failed** | Uncompetitive cost structure vs low-cost peers |
| **Outcome** | Capital-intensive, commodity business with aggressive unions and low barriers |
| **Lesson** | Avoid industries with fundamentally flawed unit economics |

### 11.4 IBM

| Factor | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **What** | Large common stock position |
| **Why sold** | Performance over six years didn't match expectations; couldn't confidently predict future against tough competition |
| **Outcome** | Sold at a loss |
| **Lesson** | Even a great company can become a poor investment at the wrong price or under competitive pressure |

