## 12. EVOLUTION OF THINKING

### 12.1 Phase 1: The Cigar Butt (1950s-1960s)

Influenced by Ben Graham:
- Buy statistically cheap, mediocre businesses trading below liquidation value
- Get one "free puff" out of a dying company
- Liquidate and move on

### 12.2 Phase 2: The Transition (1970s)

Munger convinced Buffett this strategy was:
- **Unscalable** — couldn't deploy large amounts of capital
- **Painful** — required constant liquidations
- **Inferior** — buying a great business at a fair price > terrible business at a bargain

The pivot investment: **See's Candy (1972)**

### 12.3 Phase 3: The Moat Era (1980s-Present)

Focus shifted to:
- Great businesses at fair prices
- Durable competitive advantages
- Hold "forever"
- Let compounding work

**Key evolution markers:**

| Era | What They Believed | What Changed |
|-----|-------------------|--------------|
| **1970s** | Newspapers and TV stations = "most solid investment you could find" | Moats eroded over 20-25 years |
| **1980s-90s** | Avoid tech entirely — can't predict cash flows | Made exception for Apple as consumer products brand (not tech) |
| **2000s** | Cigar butt investing rejected entirely | Now exclusively quality businesses |
| **2010s** | Stock buybacks only at 110% of book value | Eventually evolved to 120%, then to intrinsic value judgment |
| **2020** | Airlines are fine | Pandemic changed industry permanently → sold entire stakes |

