## 15. THE 20-CRITERIA INVESTMENT RUBRIC

### Scoring Scale

| Level | Label | Description |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| **1** | Reject | Fails fundamental principles; high risk of permanent capital loss or outside circle of competence |
| **2** | Weak | Subpar economics, highly capital-intensive, questionable management |
| **3** | Average | Ordinary business with fair returns, average management, no durable competitive advantage |
| **4** | Strong | Highly profitable, trustworthy management, solid margin of safety |
| **5** | Exceptional / Ideal | Flawless alignment with Buffett-Munger tenets; highly predictable, "inevitable" business with massive returns on capital |

### I. Business Understandability & Predictability

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 1 | **Circle of Competence** | Impossible to understand; highly complex technology or rapid innovation | Understandable with effort, some uncertainty | Simple enough to understand and evaluate in five minutes |
| 2 | **Rate of Change & Obsolescence** | Industry faces rapid technological disruption and constant change | Moderate rate of change | Product and consumer behavior expected to look identical 10-20 years in the future |
| 3 | **Macro-Economic Independence** | Success relies on predicting macro trends, currency rates, or interest rates | Some macro sensitivity | Agnostic to macro factors — succeeds regardless of broader economy |
| 4 | **Simplicity of Evaluation** | Requires complex math, beta models, projections to multiple decimal places | Moderately complex analysis | The investment decision "shouts at you" — obvious without higher mathematics |
| 5 | **Institutional Permanence (The "Inevitable")** | Unknown commodity, highly susceptible to shifting consumer preferences | Moderate brand loyalty | Unshakable market dominance guaranteeing survival and growth for decades |

### II. Economic Moat & Financial Characteristics

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 6 | **Durability of the Economic Moat** | No barriers to entry; pure commodity business | Moderate competitive advantages | Deep, uncrossable moat filled with "crocodiles and sharks" |
| 7 | **Return on Invested Capital (ROIC)** | Low returns (5-6%); time is enemy of the business | Adequate returns | Exceptionally high returns; can reinvest without major incremental capital |
| 8 | **Capital Intensity** | Must constantly reinvest huge sums just to stay in place | Standard capital requirements | Asset-light — "drowns in free cash flow," virtually no capital needed to grow |
| 9 | **Pricing Power & Inflation Resistance** | Price-taker; capital costs rise identically with inflation | Some pricing flexibility | Can raise prices seamlessly to offset inflation without losing volume |
| 10 | **Share of Mind / Franchise Strength** | Product lacks distinct identity or customer loyalty | Recognizable brand | Universal "share of mind" — brand automatically associated with happiness and value |

### III. Management Quality & Culture

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 11 | **Management's Passion** | Driven by money, ego, or desire to cash out | Competent professionals | Loves the business itself, treats it like family heirloom |
| 12 | **Capital Allocation Logic** | Ego-driven empire building; retains cash despite sub-par returns | Reasonable allocation | Retains earnings only if $1 retained creates >$1 market value; otherwise returns capital |
| 13 | **Integrity and Candor** | Hides bad news, manipulates earnings, creative accounting | Standard corporate communication | Outstanding integrity, reports bad news immediately, treats shareholders as partners |
| 14 | **Resistance to "Institutional Imperative"** | Mindlessly copies competitors' bad acquisitions | Some independent thinking | Thinks independently, rationally defies industry norms, avoids "lemming" mindset |
| 15 | **Cost Control & Operational Efficiency** | Bloated headquarters, excessive compensation, uncontrolled costs | Adequate cost management | Relentlessly focuses on productivity, "right-sized" operations, hates waste |

### IV. Valuation & Risk Management

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 16 | **Margin of Safety** | Priced for perfection; speculative with unlimited downside | Fair price, limited margin | Massive discount to conservatively calculated intrinsic value |
| 17 | **Discount Rate Clarity** | Requires complex risk-adjusted beta formulas | Standard WACC | Future cash flows easily clear long-term risk-free bond rate without convoluted math |
| 18 | **Accounting Transparency** | Relies on EBITDA to hide capital costs; refuses to expense options | GAAP-compliant, some fuzziness | Clean, conservative accounting reflecting real economic cash flows |
| 19 | **Opportunity Cost Test** | Fails to beat safe assets or standard index funds | Competitive with alternatives | Clearly superior to best alternative investments; justifies concentrated commitment |
| 20 | **Risk of Permanent Capital Loss** | Highly leveraged; vulnerable to single catastrophic event | Moderate debt, manageable risk | Zero or minimal leverage; structured to survive absolute worst-case scenarios |

